WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.570
What LingQ does, LingQ is a
tool, it's like training wheels.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.090
It gets you to a point where
you can now read a book and

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.650
not have to look things up.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.420
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.030
Today I want to talk about why books
are so important in language learning.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.630
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.280
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:23.240
Books.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.500
What do I mean by books?

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.660
Paper books.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:28.260
All right.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:34.640
Now you may be surprised to hear me say
that books are so important and, but I'll

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.820
tell you why I think they're important
and the role of the computer, or, you

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.880
know, iPhone, iPad, LingQ in language or
anything, and how that fits in with books.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:53.000
First of all, it's also a matter
of personal preference, but if I

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.915
have a book like this, you know,
I'm not going to be distracted.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:00:58.845
All right.

00:00:59.175 --> 00:01:00.975
I can't go and check my Twitter feed.

00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:05.055
Um, there's no lights coming at me.

00:01:05.075 --> 00:01:10.155
I'm just sort of connecting with,
with whatever is written in this

00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:15.150
book, uh, background of paper
that hopefully isn't glossy.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.830
I find that very comfortable.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.150
I enjoy doing that.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.830
I think there is research to show
that we learn better reading from a

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.360
traditional source like this, rather
than reading on a screen, even on an

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.910
iPhone screen or a computer screen.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.640
So that's one thing, it's comfortable.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.610
The second thing about books is that
it's so important in our language

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.630
learning activity, in terms of the
content that we use, in terms of the

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:47.340
different activities that we have in
terms of the different ways, you know,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.960
we approach sometimes the same material
is very important to have a variety.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.330
So I like books and I like books
where I'm able to access some of

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:01.800
the material that I've been studying
at LingQ on my iPad, for example.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:10.410
So the function of the, uh, of
LingQ specifically is to get us to

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.290
a stage where we can read a book.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:20.190
In fact, the origin of LingQ was that I
was reading books in Spanish and German,

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.380
and constantly coming across words that
I didn't know, and I would underline

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.329
them and make lists and look them up.

00:02:27.329 --> 00:02:30.990
And then I would stop looking
them up and I would forget them.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.030
And it was just very inefficient.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.530
What LingQ does, LingQ is a
tool, it's like training wheels.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.690
It gets you to a point point
where you can now read a book

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.610
and not have to look things up.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:49.130
I should add that before LingQ I used
to have readers where there would be

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:53.600
a glossary after each lesson, but here
again, it's very distracting to be

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.579
flipping back and forth to the glossary.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.230
The glossary may have the word that
you're looking for very often it doesn't.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:06.615
On the other hand, um, words they
have on their glossary, most of them

00:03:06.615 --> 00:03:09.315
are words you already know, because
there's no way the person who wrote

00:03:09.315 --> 00:03:13.215
the book can anticipate which words you
know and which words you don't know.

00:03:13.695 --> 00:03:20.055
So in a way, the, the point of LingQ is,
is to sort of bypass that stage of the

00:03:20.055 --> 00:03:24.765
reader with, uh, with the vocabulary list
and get you to the point where you can

00:03:24.975 --> 00:03:29.565
ignore the few words that you don't know,
because you now know 95% of the words.

00:03:29.655 --> 00:03:33.335
So the goal with LingQ is to
make you independent of LingQ.

00:03:34.185 --> 00:03:39.715
So, uh, I'm reminded, I think there
was a parable by Zhuangzi the Taoist,

00:03:39.735 --> 00:03:45.105
Chinese philosopher, who said something
about, uh, once the fishermen needs

00:03:45.105 --> 00:03:46.845
the fish trap to catch the fish.

00:03:46.995 --> 00:03:49.695
And once he's got the fish, he
doesn't need the fish trap anymore.

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:50.545
And then he went on to...

00:03:50.565 --> 00:03:54.915
I can't remember what it was, but
it's a typical cryptic, uh, parable.

00:03:55.305 --> 00:03:58.365
Uh, I need words for me and
once I have the meaning, I

00:03:58.365 --> 00:03:59.355
don't need the words anymore.

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:00.375
Something to that effect.

00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:01.455
I should've looked it up.

00:04:01.725 --> 00:04:02.955
In fact, I may just go look for it.

00:04:03.645 --> 00:04:07.515
Link to it from the description
box, but the point is LingQ is

00:04:07.515 --> 00:04:09.405
that kind of a, a fish trap.

00:04:09.435 --> 00:04:13.785
It's to get you to a level where
you can read in books, because

00:04:13.845 --> 00:04:17.685
to me that is the most enjoyable
way to engage with the language.

00:04:17.985 --> 00:04:20.415
Reading is for acquiring vocabulary.

00:04:21.375 --> 00:04:25.125
Uh, but listening, as I've said
before, is to get used to the language.

00:04:25.425 --> 00:04:29.745
And even there, you don't want to
always have to rely on LingQ to

00:04:29.745 --> 00:04:30.805
understand what you're listening to.

00:04:30.975 --> 00:04:34.575
You want to get to the point, I want to
get to the point where I can just go in

00:04:34.575 --> 00:04:39.795
there, pick up my podcast, understand it
all, uh, watch a movie, understand it all.

00:04:39.975 --> 00:04:46.065
So LingQ is sort of a range of tools
to get, to get you to a point where you

00:04:46.065 --> 00:04:51.600
can enjoy the language, the way native
speakers enjoy the language and, and a

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.440
goal should be to be able to read books.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:01.260
You know, I think to myself, you
know, if, if for whatever reason, the

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:07.500
technology around MP3 files and audio
recording and so forth had come first

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:13.080
before writing, and then all of a
sudden someone discovered the ability

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:18.840
to, you know, with the sort of short
annotation system, writing system to

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:23.039
record what people are saying on paper.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:24.150
Let's say we didn't have paper.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.620
We didn't have means of writing.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.210
And all of a sudden we have it, uh,
you know, would that, would people

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.140
have been interested in that or
would they just rely on, on audio?

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.550
Because after all, historically
it was the storytellers who, um,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.320
you know, kept literature alive.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.960
So if you didn't have to write
things down and you could record it,

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:43.950
would that mean you didn't need it?

00:05:44.965 --> 00:05:48.225
And I personally would still
be interested in reading.

00:05:48.225 --> 00:05:55.170
I think it's a wonderful, uh, activity
for the brain to read the symbols and

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.210
to convert these symbols into meaning.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.940
It's, it's a very demanding thing to do.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.410
It's a, quite a different type of
activity than, uh, listening to something.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:11.160
And I find that activity is most
pleasant when it is on paper, as opposed

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.410
to sort of this active screen with
the temptation to fly off elsewhere.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:21.630
So whenever I see books, um, you
know, intended for learners of the

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.020
language and eventually books, you
know, intended for native speakers,

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:27.030
I have a tendency to buy these.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.560
You'll see all the books behind me.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.850
I've got so many of these books.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.810
Surprisingly, most of them I have
read, but many I haven't yet read.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.100
And nowadays they are getting
a little better, you know, so

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.720
I bought these books on Arabic.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.910
Uh, one series is put...

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.540
I don't have the other one here,
but one is put out by Yale and

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.100
they have a place where you can go
online and get the audio recording.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:56.430
Unfortunately, they don't give you,
you can't download the audio file.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.050
You can just listen to it online.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.560
I can listen to it on my
iPad while I exercise in the

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.270
morning, but I can't download it

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.200
.
Also, they don't give
you the electronic text.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:11.219
And that would, of course enable
me to save words and phrases so I

00:07:11.219 --> 00:07:16.530
could import it to LingQ, go through
it with my training wheels on, at

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.349
LingQ, acquire the words and phrases.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:24.659
And then again, read it in the book,
which is sort of reinforcing it, doing,

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:30.120
covering the same material, but in a
different format, which is a conventional

00:07:30.179 --> 00:07:33.120
paper book, but they don't do that.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:38.700
And that's been my experience elsewhere
that books like this, they will never

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:44.190
facilitate the learning of the language
through their material by enabling you to

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:49.920
access, uh, eText or download audio files.

00:07:50.159 --> 00:07:54.390
However, another one, ah, here
we are this one, uh, because

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.159
I'm into Egypt and Arabic.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.460
So I found this other series that is
put out by the university of Cairo.

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:05.505
And they've got a tremendous
range, uh, number of audio

00:08:05.505 --> 00:08:07.635
files, which I can download.

00:08:08.145 --> 00:08:14.235
Uh, I, I can't access the, uh,
eText for these, uh, lessons, but I

00:08:14.235 --> 00:08:17.175
have ordered the books from Amazon.

00:08:17.565 --> 00:08:21.750
So I'm going to be listening
to the Egyptian Arabic audio

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:26.160
files, and then I'll be looking
forward to reading them on paper.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:33.720
Not ideal because as I say, they, the
goal is to read on paper, but I need the

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:38.460
training wheels to get my vocabulary up
to a level where I can enjoy the books.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.830
So I think I sent them an email
saying, can I, any way you could

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:46.730
send me the Etext for these,
but I don't expect to hear back.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:52.079
Cause every time I, uh, emailed these
publishers, I also emailed the Yale, uh,

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.250
bookstore and said any way I can get the
eText and the audio files, and I don't

00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:02.310
expect to hear back, but even without
that, I find it very enjoyable to read

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.079
books in the languages that I'm learning.

00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:11.700
Even if I have to devise strategies and,
and search for resources online, uh, to

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.100
power me up to a point where I can really,
you know, enjoy those books, but enjoying

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:23.490
books, real books printed on paper is
not only a goal, but I think it takes you

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:26.190
to that next level in language learning.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:32.190
So I don't think that electronic
books, Kindles, e-books are going

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:36.000
to make, uh, paper books disappear.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.475
So I wanted to talk a bit about why
books are important in language learning

00:09:41.565 --> 00:09:43.875
as well as reading for enjoyment.

00:09:44.595 --> 00:09:45.045
Bye for now.

